                    briefing_text_r1:       `Two things land in the same week: an AACSB <strong>“areas of concern”</strong> memo and a signed 6,2 Mio. € custom program. Build an honest Quality & Capacity Map. Two of twelve programs have slipped below the SA / SA+PA qualified-faculty bars; the 22 most research-active professors run at an average <strong>1,34×</strong> load once exec days are counted, with six above 1,5×; and the exec portfolio — now 31% of revenue — has <em>essentially no assurance-of-learning loop</em> despite an NPS of 72. The deliverable distinguishes a <strong>staffing problem</strong> (hire / adjuncts / substitution) from a <strong>governance problem</strong> (no AoL loop, mis-counted loads) — because the two demand different remedies.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Allocate: faculty across the portfolio`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Allocate the 88 core faculty plus an adjunct budget across the degree programs and Executive Education for the coming year, holding to a fixed personnel envelope. The constraint bites immediately: there is <strong>not enough senior research capacity</strong> to cover the degree qualification thresholds <em>and</em> the exec growth plan. Keep every program at or above threshold, protect research time for tenure-track faculty, and decide what to substitute — PA-qualified practitioners, new SA hires (time-to-hire ~11 months), co-teaching, or a research buy-out. Push too many seniors into exec and a degree program drops below the bar; lean too hard on adjuncts and your best scholars end up teaching only exec.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Decide: the 6,2 Mio. € program & the QA standard`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Take the go / no-go / re-scope decision on the DAX custom program (~2,6 Mio. € contribution, launch in 16 weeks, eight senior professors) <em>and</em> set a school-wide quality-assurance standard the accreditors will accept. Accepting at full scope books the logo but overdraws faculty already at 1,34×; declining protects them but forgoes the contribution; re-scoping — phased cohorts, blended delivery, a designated academic director plus practitioner faculty, embedded AoL — captures most of the value at a sustainable cost. Then set a <strong>minimum AoL standard</strong>: learning goals, at least one direct measure per program, a closing-the-loop cadence — proportionate enough not to collapse the 42% exec margin. And set the <strong>load ceiling</strong> that governs attrition.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Defend: the Rectorate & Accreditation brief`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present a 4-minute brief to the Rector and the chair of the Accreditation Steering Committee. Show the portfolio is staffed to standard, the QA system is credible and <em>evidenced</em>, the faculty model is sustainable (no professor structurally above the agreed ceiling), and the financial plan still delivers a surplus. The chair will probe the trade-offs: what was sacrificed, what happens if a key professor still leaves in month three, and how the plan reads to AACSB and EQUIS reviewers. Above all, table the <strong>governance rule</strong> — the documented principle that decides who teaches what when exec demand exceeds senior supply next year, and the year after.`,

                    r1_diagnosis_label:     `What is the core nature of this crisis?`,
                    r1_priority_label:      `Where is the most dangerous single exposure right before the EQUIS visit (8 months) and the AACSB review (14 months)?`,
                    r1_honesty_label:       `How honestly do you count exec teaching days against faculty workload in the diagnosis? (%)`,
                    r1_honesty_readout:     `Share of exec days counted against the workload norm:`,
                    r1_pct:                 `%`,
                    r1_honesty_hint:        `Counting exec days honestly reveals the real over-utilization (the 1.34x load and the 6 professors above 1.5x). Under-counting hides it — until a professor leaves in month three.`,
                    diag_both:              `Two distinct problems at once — a staffing gap (hire/substitute) AND a governance gap (no AoL loop, mis-counted loads) needing different remedies`,
                    diag_staffing:          `A staffing problem — we are simply short of qualified faculty; post the SA lines and hire our way out`,
                    diag_margin:            `A revenue opportunity — exec is the highest-margin unit; the answer is to lean into it and fund the gaps later`,
                    diag_reporting:         `A reporting problem — assemble the documentation the AACSB team wants and the concerns memo goes away`,
                    prio_cascade:           `A sub-threshold + no-AoL exec portfolio producing a conditions outcome at EQUIS that cascades into the AACSB review and is publicly visible to clients and ranking bodies`,
                    prio_logo:              `The risk of losing the 6,2 Mio. € DAX logo — decline it and the school looks weak to corporate clients`,
                    prio_nps:               `Nothing urgent — exec NPS is 72, the school runs a 9% surplus; the memo is a formality`,
                    prio_hire:              `Only the open SA lines — fill them and SA/PA coverage is restored`,

                    r2_seniorexec_label:    `Senior-research professors assigned to Executive Education (of 22 over-loaded seniors)`,
                    r2_seniorexec_hint:     `Exec is high-margin but consumes scarce senior capacity. Push too many seniors into exec and degree programs slip below threshold and your best scholars teach only exec; pull too few and the exec growth plan starves.`,
                    r2_adjunct_label:       `Adjunct / practitioner budget to deploy (€M, drawn from ~140 available)`,
                    adj_low:                `0,5 Mio. € — minimal adjunct use; rely on core faculty and accept tighter coverage`,
                    adj_mid:                `1,5 Mio. € — moderate adjunct support to cover degree gaps and free senior research time`,
                    adj_high:               `3,0 Mio. € — heavy adjunct/practitioner reliance to protect margin and senior availability for exec`,
                    r2_substitute_label:    `How do you cover the degree-program qualification gaps?`,
                    sub_mix:                `A governed mix — co-teaching, targeted PA practitioners, a research buy-out, and SA lines started now, keeping every program at threshold`,
                    sub_iponly:             `Move core courses to IP-qualified practitioners — cheapest, frees seniors for exec`,
                    sub_hireonly:           `Post new SA lines and wait — treat it purely as a hiring problem (time-to-hire ~11 months)`,
                    r2_research_label:      `Protect tenure-track research time? (%)`,
                    r2_research_readout:    `Research time ring-fenced for tenure-track faculty:`,

                    r3_program_label:       `The 6,2 Mio. € DAX custom program — go / no-go / re-scope (launch in 16 weeks, ~2,6 Mio. € contribution)`,
                    prog_rescope:           `Re-scope — phased cohorts, blended delivery, a designated academic director plus practitioner faculty, embedded AoL by design`,
                    prog_full:              `Accept at full scope as sold — book the 2,6 Mio. € and the DAX logo, staff it with the eight seniors`,
                    prog_decline:           `Decline — protect faculty and standards, forgo the contribution and the marquee client`,
                    r3_aol_label:           `Set the minimum assurance-of-learning standard for the exec portfolio`,
                    aolstd_proportionate:   `Proportionate — learning goals, at least one direct measure per program, and a closing-the-loop cadence`,
                    aolstd_heavy:           `Maximal — four assessments and two extra faculty days per exec program for airtight measurement`,
                    aolstd_none:            `None for now — exec NPS is 72; keep the AoL effort on degree programs only`,
                    r3_aol_hint:            `Accreditors want evidence of learning, not maximum measurement. A heavy loop can drop exec contribution margin from 42% toward 19% — collapsing the unit that funds research and scholarships.`,
                    r3_ceiling_label:       `Set the maximum sustainable senior-faculty workload ceiling (× of 100% norm)`,
                    r3_ceiling_hint:        `Six professors are above 1,5×; three won't renew exec commitments and one has an external offer. A ceiling above 1,3× keeps the over-draw that drives attrition; a ceiling at or below it costs capacity now but protects the people the plan depends on.`,

                    r4_governance_label:    `The governance rule you table to the Rectorate for resolving exec-vs-degree faculty demand when it recurs`,
                    gov_policy:             `A documented academic-governance policy — qualification thresholds and a load ceiling are protected first; exec growth is staffed only from the remaining capacity`,
                    gov_margin:             `Margin decides — when exec demand exceeds senior supply, the highest-contribution claim wins`,
                    gov_adhoc:              `Case by case — the Dean's office settles each conflict as it arises, as it does now`,
                    r4_brief_label:         `How do you present the plan to the Rector and the Accreditation Steering Committee?`,
                    brief_evidenced:        `Evidenced — show coverage at threshold, the AoL loop with a direct measure, the load ceiling, and a surplus, and name what was sacrificed and the key-professor risk`,
                    brief_rosy:             `Rosy — emphasise the DAX win and the surplus; downplay the trade-offs and the attrition risk`,
                    brief_defensive:        `Defensive — argue the concerns memo is overstated and the school is fundamentally fine`,
                    r4_contingency_label:   `Plan for the contingency that a key senior professor still leaves in month three?`,
                    cont_absorb:            `Yes — co-teaching pairs, a started SA pipeline and adjunct slack so the model absorbs one departure without breaching threshold`,
                    cont_hope:              `Lightly — note the risk in the brief but build no slack into the model`,
                    cont_ignore:            `No — the plan depends on the specific eight; assume they stay for the year`,
                    r4_conviction_label:    `Conviction: how firmly do you hold that academic quality is a system to be governed, not a by-product of the budget? (%)`,

                    warn_honesty_range:     `The exec-days honesty share must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_seniorexec_range:  `Seniors assigned to exec must be between 0 and 22.`,
                    warn_adjunct_value:     `The adjunct budget cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_research_range:    `Protected research time must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_ceiling_range:     `The load ceiling must be between 1,00× and 2,00× of the norm.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_both:          `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> This is two problems wearing one face: a staffing gap you can hire and substitute your way out of, and a governance gap — no AoL loop, exec days never counted — that no amount of hiring fixes. Treat them separately and you can apply the right remedy to each.`,
                    coach_r1_staffing:      `<strong>“We're just short of faculty” is Common Error number two.</strong> Time-to-hire for a research-active professor here is 11 months; your AACSB review is in 14. Even if you hire, what is your assurance-of-learning evidence for the exec portfolio that's now a third of revenue? Reviewers will ask for the loop, not the headcount.`,
                    coach_r1_margin:        `<strong>Leaning into the highest-margin unit is exactly how you got here.</strong> The Dean's office has been funding growth on the back of the same senior professors. Exec margin is real — but if you let the budget logic decide who teaches what, the accreditation hole deepens while you celebrate the contribution.`,
                    coach_r1_reporting:     `<strong>This is not a documentation exercise.</strong> You can assemble every binder the team asks for, but if the exec portfolio has no learning goals, no direct measure and no closed loop, the reviewers will see a satisfied-customer survey, not assurance of learning. NPS of 72 is not AoL.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named it as two problems AND saw the real exposure — a sub-threshold, no-AoL exec portfolio producing a conditions outcome at EQUIS that cascades into AACSB and is visible to your corporate clients. That's the lens that lets the next three decisions actually fix the right things.`,
                    coach_r1_nps:           `<strong>“The memo is a formality” is the most expensive sentence in this room.</strong> NPS of 72 and a 9% surplus are exactly why the quality system was allowed to lapse. A conditions letter from EQUIS in eight months is public, and corporate clients read it. This is urgent.`,
                    coach_r1_undercount:    `<strong>If you don't count the exec days, you can't see the over-utilization.</strong> On paper everyone is at 100%; in reality six professors are above 1,5× and three won't renew. Under-counting the load is how last year's plan looked fine — right up until someone resigned in month three.`,
                    coach_r2_mix:           `<strong>A governed mix is the defensible allocation.</strong> Co-teaching, targeted PA practitioners, an SA pipeline started now and a research buy-out keeps every program at threshold without betting the whole portfolio on people you're overdrawing. That holds up in front of a peer-review team.`,
                    coach_r2_iponly:        `<strong>You covered the threshold by moving core courses to practitioners.</strong> Two programs now sit below the SA+PA bar — and your most research-active faculty are teaching only exec. What signal does that send about which students get your best scholars? That's the equity question the reviewers will ask.`,
                    coach_r2_hireonly:      `<strong>Posting SA lines and waiting is a bet against the clock.</strong> Time-to-hire is 11 months and your reviews are 8 and 14 months out. Hiring is part of the answer, but if it's the whole answer your coverage is still below threshold when EQUIS arrives.`,
                    coach_r2_adjunct:       `<strong>Heavy adjunct reliance protects margin and breaks the threshold.</strong> You've pushed core teaching to IP-qualified practitioners until two programs slipped below 90% SA+PA. The margin looks better this year; the conditions letter arrives next year.`,
                    coach_r2_research:      `<strong>You've cut protected research time to almost nothing.</strong> That feeds straight into your SA classification and your attrition risk — tenure-track faculty without research time stop being SA, and start looking for the door. Protecting research time is protecting accreditation.`,
                    coach_r3_rescope:       `<strong>Re-scoping is the move that holds the whole thing together.</strong> Phased cohorts, blended delivery, an academic director plus practitioner faculty and embedded AoL captures most of the contribution without overdrawing the eight seniors — and it builds the learning loop into the program rather than bolting it on. That is academic governance, not damage control.`,
                    coach_r3_full:          `<strong>You've booked 2,6 Mio. € of contribution and a DAX logo. Now staff it.</strong> Your eight senior professors are already at 1,34× — three said they won't renew, one has an outside offer. Which degree program do you pull them out of, and what does that do to your SA coverage right before the EQUIS visit in eight months?`,
                    coach_r3_decline:       `<strong>Declining protects faculty and standards — but it's not free.</strong> You've forgone ~2,6 Mio. € of contribution and a marquee client. That can be the right call; just be sure you turned it down for a governance reason you can defend, not because re-scoping was too much work.`,
                    coach_r3_aolprop:       `<strong>That's a proportionate loop.</strong> Learning goals, one direct measure per program and a closing-the-loop cadence is credible evidence of learning the reviewers will accept — without the four-assessment overhead that collapses the margin. Accreditors want the loop, not maximum measurement.`,
                    coach_r3_aolheavy:      `<strong>Your QA standard adds four assessments and two faculty days to every exec program.</strong> Contribution margin just fell from 42% toward 19% — the unit that funds your research and scholarships. Accreditors want evidence of learning, not maximum measurement. What's the minimum credible loop?`,
                    coach_r3_aolnone:       `<strong>“NPS is 72, skip the exec AoL” is the gap the reviewers will find.</strong> The exec portfolio is a third of your revenue with no learning goals and no direct measure. High satisfaction is not assurance of learning — and an unevidenced third of revenue is exactly what a conditions letter cites.`,
                    coach_r3_ceiling:       `<strong>A ceiling above 1,5× isn't a ceiling.</strong> Six professors are already there; setting the limit above the problem just blesses the over-draw. Your plan keeps people above the line “just for this year” — that's what last year's plan said too, right before one of them left.`,
                    coach_r4_policy:        `<strong>That's the rule I needed to see.</strong> Protect qualification thresholds and the load ceiling first, then staff exec growth from the remaining capacity. A documented policy the Rectorate can stand behind is what turns this from a one-off rescue into governance that survives next year's exec demand.`,
                    coach_r4_margin:        `<strong>“Margin decides” is the policy that created the problem.</strong> If the highest-contribution claim always wins the senior professor, your degree thresholds and your research time are residuals — and the accreditation hole reopens the moment the next big exec deal lands.`,
                    coach_r4_adhoc:         `<strong>Case-by-case is how you got the mis-counted loads and the lapsed AoL loop.</strong> When the Dean's office settles each conflict ad hoc, the budget wins quietly every time. Show me the rule, not the next negotiation.`,
                    coach_r4_rosy:          `<strong>A rosy brief that hides the trade-offs doesn't survive the room.</strong> The Rector and the chair already know about the over-loaded faculty and the concerns memo. Lead with the DAX win and bury the attrition risk, and the first probing question unravels the whole presentation.`,
                    coach_r4_defensive:     `<strong>Arguing the memo is overstated is the worst posture.</strong> The peer-review team wrote it; telling the chair they're wrong reads as denial. The credible move is to concede the gap and show the closed loop and the staffed thresholds that fix it.`,
                    coach_r4_contingency:   `<strong>Your plan keeps depending on people you're quietly overdrawing.</strong> One leaves in month three — and your accreditation coverage, your custom program and your research output all wobble at once. Where is the slack, and who absorbs the departure without breaching threshold?`,
                    coach_r4_defended:      `<strong>That brief withstands the room.</strong> Coverage at threshold, an evidenced AoL loop, a real load ceiling, a surplus, AND a documented governance rule — plus an honest account of what you sacrificed and how the model absorbs a departure. That reads to AACSB and EQUIS as a school that governs quality as a system.`,

