                    insight_secured:        `Accreditation secured: you governed quality as a system — a re-scoped program that protected the faculty, a proportionate AoL loop the reviewers can verify, coverage held at threshold, and a surplus intact. The EQUIS and AACSB visits read as a school in control of its own standards.`,
                    insight_burnout:        `Faculty burnout: the plan kept senior professors structurally over the ceiling. With three unwilling to renew and one holding an outside offer, a departure in month three takes accreditation coverage, the custom program and research output with it — capacity is only renewable if it's governed.`,
                    insight_belowthreshold: `Below threshold: programs slipped under the SA / SA+PA bars — usually by over-pushing seniors into exec or over-relying on practitioners. That's the finding that produces a conditions outcome at EQUIS and cascades into the AACSB review.`,
                    insight_short:          `You didn't fully reconcile the four decisions. Capacity, the program, the AoL loop and the governance rule still pull in slightly different directions — enough for a peer-review team to find the seam.`,
                    insight_logo:           `Chasing the logo cost you the faculty. Booking the DAX contribution without adding capacity overdrew the exact people accreditation, the program and research all depend on — the hidden faculty price of exec revenue, paid in attrition risk.`,
                    insight_adjunct:        `Adjunct over-reliance protected margin and broke the threshold. Pushing core courses to practitioners until programs slipped below the bar — while your best scholars taught only exec — is the equity-and-quality trap the reviewers probe.`,
                    insight_noaol:          `The quality system stayed thin: too little of a closed assurance-of-learning loop for an exec portfolio that's a third of revenue. High NPS is not AoL, and an unevidenced third of revenue is what a conditions letter cites.`,
                    insight_margin:         `The surplus is under strain — either the program was declined or under-scoped, or the AoL loop was over-built until it crushed the 42% exec margin. The unit that funds research and scholarships has to survive the quality system, not be destroyed by it.`,
                    insight_system:         `You made faculty allocation, qualification standards and assurance-of-learning explicit and defensible — quality as a governed system rather than an emergent by-product of the budget. That's the disciplinary point, and it's what a documented governance rule protects next year.`,

                    hint_overdraw:          `Faculty sustainability is breaking — professors are structurally over the ceiling. Re-scope or decline the program, lower the ceiling, and build slack so a departure doesn't breach threshold.`,
                    hint_below:             `Programs are below the SA/PA threshold. Pull seniors back from exec into degree coverage, ease off adjuncts, and use a governed mix so every program clears the bar.`,
                    hint_adjunct:           `You're over-relying on adjuncts to protect margin — programs are slipping below threshold and your scholars are teaching only exec. Re-balance toward a governed mix.`,
                    hint_aol:               `The assurance-of-learning loop is too thin for a third of revenue. Set a proportionate AoL standard — learning goals, one direct measure, a closing-the-loop cadence — before the EQUIS visit.`,
                    hint_margin:            `The surplus is thin. Re-scope the program to capture contribution and keep the AoL loop proportionate — credible evidence without four assessments per program.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — accreditation, the quality loop and faculty sustainability all healthy. Lock it in with a documented governance rule and an evidenced brief.`,

